664 0 obj
<</Title(The Analysis :  Sales data is in the order_items table, while category names are in the products table. I joined them on product_id and grouped the results by category. For each category I summed sale_price, counted item rows and averaged retail_price, then sorted by sales and kept the top five.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 517 0 R/Prev 700 0 R/Next 604 0 R>>
endobj

665 0 obj
<</Title(Categories of the 20 products furthest above their category average)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 605 0 R/First 701 0 R/Last 702 0 R/Count -6>>
endobj

666 0 obj
<</Title(4\) First-time vs Repeat)/Dest[14 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 605 0 R/First 703 0 R/Last 704 0 R/Count -12/Next 606 0 R>>
endobj

667 0 obj
<</Title(category)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 606 0 R/Next 705 0 R>>
endobj

668 0 obj
<</Title(Insight  Returns cost about $1.06M, or 9.98% of sales. The rate is similar in almost every category \(9 to 10%\), so this is a company-wide issue, not one bad category. The money sits in the big sellers: Jeans and Outerwear make up 23% of returned value. Suits has the highest rate at 11.5%, but from a small base. Suggestion : We would start with fit and quality checks on Jeans and Outerwear and find which need to launch new model ?)/Dest[15 0 R/Fit]/Parent 606 0 R/Prev 706 0 R>>
endobj

669 0 obj
<</Title(users)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 518 0 R/Prev 607 0 R/Next 707 0 R>>
endobj

670 0 obj
<</Title(The Analysis :  For each customer I count the days since their latest order and group them as active \(30 days or less\), passive \(31 to 90\) or churned \(over 90\). Then I count customers per group by country and calculate the percentages.)/Dest[16 0 R/Fit]/Parent 518 0 R/Prev 708 0 R/Next 608 0 R>>
endobj

